# House style

## Principles

| Principle | Expectation |
|-----------|-------------|
| **S**ingle responsibility | One reason to change per module/function |
| **O**pen/closed | Extend via packages/modules/hooks; avoid editing core paths for tenant features |
| **L**iskov | Adapters (Firestore / in-memory) honor the same repository contracts |
| **I**nterface segregation | Prefer narrow ports over god repositories |
| **D**ependency inversion | Apps depend on `@repo/*` contracts; wire concrete adapters in `server.ts` / worker entrypoints |

Also: **DRY** (extract only after 2+ real uses), **KISS**, **YAGNI** (no speculative abstractions).

## Complexity (Big-O)

Flag and fix in hot paths (API handlers, list queries, workers, UI list renders):

- N+1 Firestore / HTTP (prefer `getAll`, batch, or join/query redesign)
- Unbounded scans / missing pagination
- Sync `await` inside tight loops when batch/parallel is safe
- Accidental `O(n²)` over large collections

Prefer cursor pagination (default limit 20, max 100). See `docs/guides/performance-scaling.md`.

## Code shape

- Small pure functions; inject dependencies instead of module-scoped singletons
- Discriminated unions + exhaustive `switch` (`never` default) for variants
- Structured errors with stable codes (API `{ data, error }` envelope)
- No dead code; no comments that restate the obvious
- Prefer editing existing patterns over inventing parallel stacks

## Do not

- Add tenant-specific business logic into shared packages (use dynamic entities, hooks, or `.local/` data)
- Bypass package boundaries (e.g. apps importing Firestore SDK directly)
- Ship unbounded list endpoints or unpaginated admin dumps

## Enforcement

- `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm lint`, `pnpm lint:duplicates`, `pnpm lint:circular`
- Reviewers and agents: refuse N+1 / unbounded patterns in new code

